+# Hegel property-based testing report — `jotdown`
+
+Target: `jotdown` v0.10.0 (hellux/jotdown), a pull parser for the Djot markup
+language. Tests added with hegel (crate `hegeltest`, imported as `hegel`) in
+`tests/properties.rs` (a new test target, mirroring the repo's one-file-per-
+concern layout under `tests/`). No production code was changed.
+
+## 1. What was tested
+
+Nine live properties plus one ignored process-abort reproducer. The emphasis is
+on **oracle-independent** structural invariants — a panic, an abort, an
+out-of-range span, or a violated self-evident invariant needs no external
+source of truth to be trusted as a bug.
+
+| # | Test | Property | Kind |
+|---|------|----------|------|
+| 1 | `parse_never_panics_arbitrary_text` | `Parser::new(s)`, both iterator forms fully consumed, never panics on arbitrary `String` | no-panic |
+| 2 | `parse_never_panics_djot_shaped` | Same, on documents assembled from ~40 real Djot fragments (headings, lists, emphasis, links, code blocks, tables, divs, spans, footnotes, attributes, math, symbols) | no-panic |
+| 3 | `deep_nesting_bounded_no_abort` | Sweeps nesting depth 0..=120 across blockquote / div / list / emphasis / span, biased deeper via `tc.target`; emitted-event count is capped so a superlinear/runaway emission becomes a falsifiable failure rather than an OOM | no-abort / bounded |
+| 4 | `event_balance_arbitrary_text`, `event_balance_djot_shaped` | Every `Start` has a matching `End` of the same `Container` kind, closed in LIFO order; no unclosed containers remain | structural invariant |
+| 5 | `spans_valid_arbitrary_text`, `spans_valid_djot_shaped` | Every event's source range is within the input, on char boundaries, and non-overlapping/in-order (with the parser's documented Caption exception); only whitespace follows the final event | structural invariant |
+| 6 | `html_render_never_panics_and_deterministic` | `html::Renderer` never panics and produces byte-identical output on repeated runs | no-panic + determinism |
+| 7 | `render_apis_agree` | `html::render_to_string` == `Renderer::default().push_events` for the same events | sibling-API agreement |
+| 8 | `str_content_is_subsequence_arbitrary`, `str_content_is_subsequence_djot` | Concatenated `Event::Str` content is an in-order char subsequence of the source (escapes only drop the backslash; smart punctuation is emitted as dedicated non-`Str` events) | text preservation |
+| 9 | `attributes_parse_never_panics_arbitrary`, `attributes_parse_never_panics_shaped` | `Attributes::try_from` returns `Err` (never panics) on arbitrary and attribute-block-shaped input; inline attributes in a full document also parse without panic | no-panic |
+
+Properties 4 and 5 deliberately re-encode the invariants from the crate's own
+AFL target (`tests/afl/src/lib.rs::parse`), but drive them from generated
+Djot-shaped input and from the arbitrary-text path, rather than only from
+fuzzer corpus bytes.
+
+## 2. Bugs found
+
+### CONFIRMED (process-aborting): stack overflow on deep block nesting
+
+The block parser (`src/block.rs`: `parse_block` → `parse_container` →
+`parse_block`) recurses **once per nested block container** with no depth
+limit. A small, fully attacker-controlled input therefore overflows the stack
+and **aborts the process** (SIGABRT — not a catchable panic).
+
+Measured thresholds on cargo's default 2 MiB spawned-test-thread stack:
+
+- `> ` blockquote nesting aborts between depth **400 and 500** (input ≈ 1 KB)
+- `::: ` div nesting aborts between depth **460 and 480** (input ≈ 2 KB)
+- Nested lists share the same recursion but the O(d²) source size makes them
+  slower to reach the limit.
+
+This is an untrusted-input denial-of-service: roughly 1 KB of input can crash
+any service that parses Djot from an untrusted source. Because it aborts rather
+than panics, a permanently-failing test would take the whole suite down, so it
+is pinned by an **`#[ignore]`d** reproducer, `deep_nesting_stack_overflow`
+(uses `"> ".repeat(1000)`, comfortably past both thresholds), with the analysis
+in the test's comment block and this report. Confirmed to abort with SIGABRT
+when run via `cargo test --test properties deep_nesting_stack_overflow --
+--ignored`.
+
+Per the task's ground rules the bug was **not fixed** (a fix would add an
+explicit recursion-depth limit returning an error, as many hardened parsers
+do).
+
+### OBSERVED (not pinned): superlinear parse time on some nested constructs
+
+Several single-construct nestings parse in **O(n²)** time in the input length
+before (or without) overflowing:
+
+- Nested divs: depth 100 → 4 ms, 200 → 16 ms, 400 → 64 ms (source grows O(d)).
+- Nested spans `[[[…]]]` / bracket runs `[[[[`: depth 8000 → ~0.28 s, 16000 →
+  ~1.1 s, 32000 → ~4.5 s (source grows O(d)).
+
+Spans and brackets do not overflow the stack (they are handled iteratively) but
+are quadratic in time — a milder DoS vector. This is documented here but not
+pinned as a failing test, because there is no clean deterministic time-cap
+oracle; property 3's event-count cap guards against the runaway-*emission*
+failure mode within the safe depth range.
+
+No other invariant violations were found. The no-panic, event-balance,
+span-validity, determinism, render-API-agreement, Str-subsequence, and
+attribute-robustness properties all held.
+
+## 3. How confident we are / oracle notes
+
+- **High confidence** in properties 1–9: all are oracle-independent (panics,
+  aborts, structural invariants, self-comparison) except property 7, whose
+  "oracle" is a sibling API of the same crate — a weak oracle, but the two
+  paths are genuinely distinct entry points, so agreement is still meaningful.
+- The **stack overflow is oracle-independent and reproduced by construction**
+  (a fixed input aborts every run) — it is a real defect, not an oracle
+  artifact.
+- The **Str-subsequence** property was validated against escapes, HTML
+  entities, smart punctuation, autolinks, code spans, and attributes before
+  committing; smart punctuation (en/em dash, ellipsis, quotes, non-breaking
+  space) is emitted as dedicated `Event` variants, never as `Str`, so the
+  subsequence claim is sound.
+
+## 4. Liveness and exploratory verification
+
+- **Mutation/liveness:** three distinct properties were temporarily broken and
+  each produced a shrunk counterexample, then reverted:
+  - `event_balance_djot_shaped` (rejected `Paragraph`) → shrank to `"plain text\n"`.
+  - `str_content_is_subsequence_djot` (required empty Str) → shrank to `"# heading\n"`.
+  - `spans_valid_djot_shaped` (required empty spans) → shrank to `"# heading\n"`.
+  This confirms the assertions are reachable and the generators feed real input
+  (the guarded branches in properties 4/5/8 fire on the fragment documents).
+- **Exploratory high-count run:** all 13 live properties run at
+  `test_cases = 1500` (≈15× the default). A per-case atomic counter confirmed
+  **1582 cases** actually executed for the representative property; the whole
+  suite stayed green (`13 passed; 1 ignored`). Instrumentation was reverted
+  afterward.
+- **No-regression:** the full crate test suite (`cargo test`) passes —
+  99 + 18 + 6 + 79 + 49 + 13 + 83 tests across all targets, `0 failed`.
+
+## 5. Setup notes / gotchas
+
+- Added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest -p jotdown --ignore-rust-version`. The
+  crate pins `rust-version = "1.82"`; hegeltest 0.28.2 requires rustc 1.86, so
+  the MSRV override was required. **If these tests are upstreamed, the
+  dev-dependency raises the effective test MSRV to 1.86** — that is the
+  maintainer's call to accept.
+- `.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+- `tests/properties.rs` is its own test target; it is intentionally **not**
+  added as a `mod` in `tests/lib.rs` (doing so would double-compile it, as
+  happens for the existing `attr`/`cli`/`html`/`parse_events` files).
+- Maintainer AI-contribution consent: no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, `.ai-ack`, or
+  anti-AI clause was found in `CONTRIBUTING`/`README`/`LICENSE`/`COPYING`.
+  Work was kept local and **not pushed**, per the task's ground rules; the
+  decision to upstream (and to accept the raised MSRV) is the user's.
